WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.720 align:start position:19%
even if you train at a commercial gym
microloading is still worth it

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.960 align:start position:19%
see there's a common argument that
because individual weight plates vary so

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.800 align:start position:19%
much
you can never really know what you're

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.400 align:start position:19%
actually lifting the sum is going to be
off

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.680 align:start position:19%
100 of the time unless using calibrated
plates

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.039 align:start position:19%
and that does pose a huge problem for
not only

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.600 align:start position:19%
adding small plates but also tracking in
general

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.279 align:start position:19%
especially when the gym is massive and
you got guys

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.199 align:start position:19%
mixing and matching the plates or
bringing them from one side

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.360 align:start position:19%
to the other this means that on any
given workout day

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:40.239 align:start position:19%
you are not using the same plates as the
previous session

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:46.160 align:start position:19%
sounds horrific right except for a few
things i'd like to point out

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:50.399 align:start position:19%
the first is the brand of the plates
not all them are created equal even if

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.680 align:start position:19%
they are the basic cast iron ones
for example if you go on the rogue

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:57.680 align:start position:19%
fitness website
and check out the standard olympic

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.199 align:start position:19%
plates you'll see that they indicate a
plus or minus

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:05.199 align:start position:19%
three percent tolerance which is a lot
better compared to

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:08.240 align:start position:19%
the cheaper brands this is what really
accounts

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.280 align:start position:19%
for those crazy fluctuations the
muscular imbalances that you've seen

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.479 align:start position:19%
so often that's not body related you go
to do a bench press and you completely

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.479 align:start position:19%
twist to decide what is going on well
it's not your fault

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.439 align:start position:19%
it's just happened to me too so it's
important to do your research before

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.920 align:start position:19%
selecting those plays because a lot of
gems

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:32.000 align:start position:19%
have different kinds available in my
case there were three sets

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:36.479 align:start position:19%
the standard olympia and rubbers
what i found over time is that the

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.159 align:start position:19%
olympias were true to their weight
and what's really cool is that my friend

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.280 align:start position:19%
who used to work at the gym and i would
work out with him too

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.320 align:start position:19%
would always tell me alex use the
olympias

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:50.640 align:start position:19%
why because on his own free time he
weighed them all out and found them to

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.960 align:start position:19%
be as close as possible to 45 pounds
whereas the standard would often be a

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:58.479 align:start position:19%
little bit heavier or slightly lighter
so from that point forward i always use

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.439 align:start position:19%
the olympias and my form look that much
cleaner

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:08.080 align:start position:19%
and when i would track the workouts in
my logbook which i still do

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:12.239 align:start position:19%
to the day old school bro the
numbers made sense

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.040 align:start position:19%
which is another point i want to throw
in there

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:23.200 align:start position:19%
even if it is true that
it's not really accurate

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:28.879 align:start position:19%
what remains consistent
rpe if you're good at using it

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:31.280 align:start position:19%
and a lot of experienced lifters
incorporate that as a major part of

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.480 align:start position:19%
their training
this way you're kind of auto regulating

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.959 align:start position:19%
according to that session and hey it
might just be that you did

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:42.480 align:start position:19%
under recover slightly so you don't have
to necessarily remove the microplates to

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:46.239 align:start position:19%
accommodate for that
as long as the intensity is being

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.400 align:start position:19%
matched
it's all good also depending how you

00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:55.040 align:start position:19%
started off
with the session you're going to be

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.000 align:start position:19%
using the same plates for backup fork
and a lot of us are using that in our

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:04.159 align:start position:19%
training in fact it's one of the main
ways i got my 405 bench

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:10.239 align:start position:19%
you work up to a heavy single and then
off that one rep max stick to the

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:14.080 align:start position:19%
60 to 70 percent range so you might
remove

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.959 align:start position:19%
a plate a plate and a half depends on
the exercise

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:21.040 align:start position:19%
so in this case even if your peak weight
and back off weight was

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.879 align:start position:19%
wrong the percentage is still
mostly correct because technically the

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:27.680 align:start position:19%
plates that you removed
could have been on the heavier side

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.680 align:start position:19%
which can distort things a bit but at
least we know that it's

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:36.080 align:start position:19%
closer to our objective like we can't be
perfect

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:39.280 align:start position:19%
here let's be as reasonable as possible
when talking about tracking and micro

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:43.040 align:start position:19%
loading otherwise
let's just forget about the idea

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:46.080 align:start position:19%
completely of literally
tracking anything because then you're

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.519 align:start position:19%
just wasting your time and i think
that perspective is a little bit extreme

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:53.200 align:start position:19%
so that's my first point
the second is actually a pretty good one

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.239 align:start position:19%
that not a lot of people are talking
about

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:02.239 align:start position:19%
in my experience of going to many gyms
working out with people in person

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:06.879 align:start position:19%
talking to you guys online
in most cases and give me your feedback

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.760 align:start position:19%
in the comment section to verify what
i'm saying i want to get some more

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:14.000 align:start position:19%
examples on this
the 45 pounders are the most inaccurate

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:18.160 align:start position:19%
plates
not when you go below except for maybe

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:24.400 align:start position:19%
the 35 pounders but very few guys
use that what i have consistently found

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.360 align:start position:19%
is that the 10 pounders 9 out of 10
times weigh exactly 10 pounds when you

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.280 align:start position:19%
weigh them out
the two and a half pounders same thing

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:38.720 align:start position:19%
and if it's 2.4 who gives
up and the fives always good

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:48.080 align:start position:19%
and the 25s tend to be correct as well
the worst i've seen is like 24.6 or 24.8

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:52.320 align:start position:19%
or 25.2 but usually
they tend to be smack at 25. so i see

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:57.280 align:start position:19%
some guys who will buy
calibrated 45 and 55 pound plates

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.120 align:start position:19%
but then the rest of their setup is cast
iron why because they figured this out

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:04.720 align:start position:19%
the same way
the heavier you go up there the more

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.880 align:start position:19%
inaccurate they tend to be which
makes sense doesn't it so if you know

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:13.120 align:start position:19%
this information what does this imply
for most of your basic accessory work

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:17.360 align:start position:19%
let's say your isolations or little
small movements

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.400 align:start position:19%
microloading is a terrific idea because
it probably is

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.759 align:start position:19%
what it says or at least much closer to
